---
import Guide from '../../layouts/Guide.astro';
import Analogy from '../../components/Analogy.astro';
import LaunchLab from '../../components/LaunchLab.astro';
import { DEFAULT_LAUNCH as D } from '../../lib/launcher.ts';
---

<Guide slug="pen-launcher-physics">
  <h2>Why it matters</h2>
  <p>
    The Pen Launcher went through many prototypes. The physics below is what decided the spring, the pull-back
    distance and the angle of the barrel. It's also the fastest way to win the office distance contest.
  </p>

  <h2>The intuition</h2>
  <Analogy title="A bow and arrow">
    <p>
      Pulling back the plunger is like drawing a bow: you store energy in the spring, and releasing it pours that energy
      into the pen. Pull back twice as far and you store <strong>four times</strong> the energy, because the spring
      pushes harder the further it's stretched.
    </p>
    <p>
      Where it breaks down: a bow's string is very light, so most of the energy reaches the arrow. The launcher's
      plunger is heavier, and some energy is lost to friction, so only about half of it reaches the pen.
    </p>
  </Analogy>

  <figure class="panel dia">
    <svg viewBox="0 0 560 200" role="img" aria-label="Pen Launcher: spring, plunger, pen and launch angle">
      <desc>
        A side view of the launcher. A spring of stiffness k is compressed by the pull-back distance x behind a plunger.
        The pen sits in a barrel angled at theta above horizontal, at height h above the floor, and leaves at speed v.
      </desc>
      <line x1="20" y1="185" x2="540" y2="185" stroke="var(--line-2)"></line>
      <rect x="40" y="150" width="200" height="35" fill="var(--panel-2)" stroke="var(--line-2)"></rect>
      <text x="140" y="172" text-anchor="middle" class="lbl">desk</text>
      <g transform="rotate(-25 80 140)">
        <rect x="60" y="122" width="230" height="30" rx="6" fill="none" stroke="var(--accent)" stroke-width="2"></rect>
        <path d="M68 137 l8 -9 l8 18 l8 -18 l8 18 l8 -18 l8 18 l8 -9" fill="none" stroke="var(--text-2)" stroke-width="2"></path>
        <rect x="132" y="126" width="12" height="22" fill="var(--accent)"></rect>
        <rect x="146" y="132" width="130" height="10" rx="5" fill="var(--text)"></rect>
        <path d="M276 132 l14 5 l-14 5z" fill="var(--text-3)"></path>
        <text x="98" y="116" class="lbl">k, x</text>
        <path d="M300 137 h60" stroke="var(--accent)" stroke-width="2" marker-end="url(#arr)"></path>
        <text x="330" y="128" class="lbl">v</text>
      </g>
      <defs>
        <marker id="arr" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="8" refY="5" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto">
          <path d="M0 0L10 5L0 10z" fill="var(--accent)"></path>
        </marker>
      </defs>
      <path d="M90 140 h70" stroke="var(--text-3)" stroke-dasharray="3 3"></path>
      <path d="M140 140 A50 50 0 0 0 135 119" fill="none" stroke="var(--text-2)"></path>
      <text x="146" y="134" class="lbl">θ</text>
      <path d="M260 150 v35" stroke="var(--text-2)"></path>
      <text x="266" y="172" class="lbl">h</text>
      <path d="M420 60 Q480 70 520 185" fill="none" stroke="var(--accent)" stroke-dasharray="4 4"></path>
    </svg>
    <figcaption class="hint">Schematic, not to scale.</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <h2>The physics</h2>
  <p>Three steps:</p>
  <div class="math">{`1. Energy in the spring   E = ½ k x²
2. Speed of the pen        v = √(2ηE / m)
3. Time in the air         t = (v sinθ + √((v sinθ)² + 2gh)) / g
   Distance                range = v cosθ × t`}</div>
  <p>
    Here <strong>k</strong> is the spring's stiffness (N/m), <strong>x</strong> the pull-back (m), <strong>η</strong>
    the share of the spring's energy that reaches the pen, <strong>m</strong> the pen's mass (kg), <strong>θ</strong>
    the launch angle, <strong>h</strong> the launch height (m) and <strong>g</strong> = 9.81 m/s².
  </p>
  <p>
    On flat ground, 45° goes furthest. Launching from a desk changes that: the pen already has height to fall, so a
    flatter angle that keeps more speed going forward wins. From desk height the best angle is around 40°.
  </p>

  <h2>Try it</h2>
  <div class="wide"><LaunchLab /></div>

  <h2>Worked example: the default launch</h2>
  <p>A {D.k} N/m spring pulled back {D.drawMm} mm, {D.efficiency * 100}% efficient, a {D.penGrams} g pen, {D.angleDeg}° from a {D.heightM} m desk:</p>
  <div class="math">{`E      = ½ × 300 × 0.040²                     = 0.24 J
E_pen  = 0.5 × 0.24                           = 0.12 J
v      = √(2 × 0.12 / 0.006)                  = 6.32 m/s
v sinθ = 6.32 × sin 35°                       = 3.63 m/s
v cosθ = 6.32 × cos 35°                       = 5.18 m/s
t      = (3.63 + √(3.63² + 2 × 9.81 × 0.75)) / 9.81 = 0.908 s
range  = 5.18 × 0.908                         = 4.70 m
peak   = 0.75 + 3.63² / (2 × 9.81)            = 1.42 m`}</div>
  <p>The site's tests check these same numbers, so the tool and this page can't drift apart.</p>

  <h2>Where this stops</h2>
  <div class="stops">
    <ul>
      <li><strong>No air drag.</strong> A light pen at 6 m/s loses noticeable speed to the air, and a tumbling pen loses more. Expect real ranges 10–30% shorter.</li>
      <li><strong>Efficiency is an estimate.</strong> The 50% default is illustrative. Measure your range and adjust it until the tool matches.</li>
      <li><strong>The spring constant is illustrative.</strong> Springs vary. To measure yours, hang a known weight from it and divide the force by the stretch.</li>
      <li><strong>Safety:</strong> aim at targets, never at people, pets or faces. Don't fit stronger springs than the design was made for.</li>
    </ul>
  </div>

  <h2>References</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Any introductory mechanics text on spring potential energy and projectile motion, e.g. Halliday, Resnick &amp; Walker, <em>Fundamentals of Physics</em>, chapters 4 and 8.</li>
  </ul>
</Guide>

